  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1031 AM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nMILD AND DRY CONDITIONS THROUGH THANKSGIVING:\n\nA shortwave ridge moving overhead will lead to dry and mild\nafternoon conditions. Upstream, high clouds continue to\ninfiltrate across the area as a shortwave approaches from the\nwest. All indications continue to point towards a split flow\npattern with a closed low passing well south of Arizona and New\nMexico, leaving us with just some passing mid and high cloud\ncover this evening into Thanksgiving morning with thin high\nclouds Thanksgiving Day and pleasant mild and dry conditions\nwith temperatures around 5 to 10 degrees above normal. Some low\nlevel moisture with the colder nights may lead to patchy fog\ndevelopment overnight especially where clearing skies occur but\nlow confidence due to passing cloud cover which would make this\nless likely.\n\nPOTENTIAL SYSTEM AFTER THANKSGIVING INTO THE WEEKEND:\n\nBeyond Thanksgiving Day, moisture will begin moving into the\nPacific Northwest and across the northern Rockies Friday into\nthe weekend, setting up a northwest flow across the region as\nhigh pressure builds over the Desert Southwest just off the\nSoCal coast. The Park Range looks to receive the brunt of this\nmoisture with a favored set up for northwest orographic snowfall\nFriday through Saturday, but more particularly Friday evening\nthrough Saturday night. While confidence is high that a weather\nsystem will impact the region, there are still some\ndiscrepancies on how far south the impacts will extend based on\nvarying model solutions. We could see impacts down to Vail Pass\nespecially into Saturday as a stronger piece of energy pushes\nthrough on the back side of the trough, but the Park Range \nlooks to benefit the most from decent snow accumulations from \nthis northwest orographic flow. Confidence is highest over the \nPark Range with lowering confidence to the south. Highlights \nmay be needed but would like to see some better clarification on\ndetails before issuing any products depending on timing and \namounts. Anyone traveling late this week and this weekend \nshould prepare for potentially difficult conditions. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD TO NEXT WEEK:\n\nHigh pressure ridge moves back in for Sunday following the\npassage of this system with northwest flow setting up again on\nMonday as a weak clipper brushes to the north. There is a chance\nthat northwest orographic flow could keep snow ongoing over the\nPark Range so this remains to be seen. Looking ahead, there are\nsome indications of another system impacting the region mid to\nlate next week but guidance diverges on specific scenarios.\nRegardless, it looks to remain unsettled.",
